==English==
{{was wotd|2007|March|7}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|upbreyden}}, from {{inh|en|ang|upbreġdan}}, equivalent to {{af|en|up-|braid}}. Compare {{cog|en|umbraid|t=to upbraid}}, {{cog|is|bregða|t=to draw, brandish, braid, deviate from, change, break off, upbraid}}. See {{m|en|up}}, and {{m|en|braid}} {{qualifier|transitive}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌʌpˈbɹeɪd/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-upbraid.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪd|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[criticize]] severely.
#: {{syn|en|exprobrate|blame|censure|condemn|reproach|Thesaurus:criticize}}
#* {{RQ:Sidney Arcadia|passage=How much doth thy kindness '''upbraid''' my wickedness!}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|book=Matthew|chapter=11|verse=20|passage=Then began he to '''upbraid''' the cities wherein most of his mighty works were done, because they repented not:}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1980|title=AA Book of British Villages|publisher=Drive Publications Ltd|page=174|text=Indeed, on several occasions, the Bishop of Lincoln was forced to '''upbraid''' both abbess and nuns for unseemliness in dress and behaviour.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en-US|author={{w|John Cassidy (journalist)|John Cassidy}}|title=Mastering the Machine|work=The New Yorker|date=2011-07-18|url=https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2011/07/25/mastering-the-machine|issn=0028-792X|passage=Dalio had no qualms about '''upbraiding''' a junior employee in front of me and dozens of his colleagues.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en-US|author=Cameron Joseph|date=March 5 2025|titleurl=https://www.csmonitor.com/USA/Politics/2025/0305/trump-speech-congress-tariffs|title=After wild six weeks, Trump tells Congress ‘We are just getting started’|journal=The Christian Science Monitor|passage=Mr. Trump and Vice President JD Vance '''upbraided''' the president of Ukraine in the Oval Office last Friday, then cut off military aid to the U.S. ally.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic|followed by ''with'' or ''for'', and formerly ''of'' before the object}} To charge with something wrong or disgraceful; to [[reproach]]
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Coriolanus|V|i|passage=Yet do not '''upbraid''' us our distress.}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|book=Mark|chapter=16|verse=14|passage=Afterward he appeared unto the eleven as they sat at meat, and '''upbraided''' them with their unbelief and hardness of heart, because they believed not them which had seen him after he was risen.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To treat with [[contempt]].
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|1|5
|passage=There also was that mighty monarch laid, Low under all, yet above all in pride; That name of native fire did foul '''upbraid''', And would, as Ammon's son, be magnify'd.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|followed by "to" before the object}} To object or urge as a matter of [[reproach]]
#: {{syn|en|cast up}}
#* {{RQ:Bacon Essayes|chapter=Of Envy|passage=Those that have been bred together, are more apt to envy their equals when raised: for it doth '''upbraid''' unto them their own fortunes, and pointeth at them.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|intransitive}} To utter upbraidings.
# {{lb|en|UK|_|dialectal|Northern England|archaic}} To [[vomit]]; [[retch]].

